---
// Brand + product intent. The "how signals work" intent belongs to /javascript-signals-runtime/.
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Install from "../components/Install.astro";
import Example from "../components/Example.astro";
import BenchmarkSummary from "../components/BenchmarkSummary.astro";
import { BLOB, href } from "../site";
---
<Base
  path="/"
  title="Reflex Quant: Lightweight, High-Performance JavaScript Signals Library"
  headline="Reflex Quant"
  description="Reflex Quant is a 2.2 KB fine-grained reactivity library for JavaScript and TypeScript: signal, computed, effect, with a written contract and published benchmarks. MIT."
  faq={[
    ["What is Reflex Quant?", "A JavaScript and TypeScript signals library (signal, computed, effect) of about 2.2 KB gzip, derived as the smallest runtime a fixed reactive contract implies."],
    ["Is Reflex Quant production ready?", "It is published on npm under MIT, tested differentially against an executable specification on over 140,000 programs, and its benchmarks run on every CI build. It is also a research artifact; read the contract before relying on edge-case behaviour."],
    ["Does Reflex Quant work with React, Vue or Solid?", "Quant is framework-agnostic: it has no rendering layer. It can back state in any framework through an adapter that subscribes with effect()."],
  ]}
>
  <p class="lede">A JavaScript signals runtime of about 2.2 KB gzip: signals, computeds and effects, with exact dynamic dependency tracking, glitch-free propagation, and no recursion on any path.</p>
  <Install />
  <p>Quant is a research project as much as a library. It asks one question: what is the smallest reactive runtime that a fixed semantic contract implies? Every mechanism in it has a counterexample showing that the contract, or a structural bound, breaks without it. Nothing is in it because another framework has it.</p>

  <h2>Fine-grained reactivity in one example</h2>
  <p>A computation depends on exactly what its last run read, so dependencies change from run to run:</p>
  <Example />

  <h2>What you get</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Exact tracking.</strong> A computed observes exactly the sources it read; branches add and drop dependencies. <a href={href("/dynamic-dependency-tracking/")}>How dynamic dependency tracking works</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Glitch freedom.</strong> One execution per node per change, diamonds included.</li>
    <li><strong>Stack safety.</strong> Propagation and validation use one explicit work stack, so chain depth is bounded by memory, not by the JavaScript call stack.</li>
    <li><strong>Defined failure semantics.</strong> A computed that throws commits nothing and runs again on its next demand.</li>
    <li><strong>Executable specification.</strong> Equal to the reference spec on 27,061 bounded programs, 18,660 recovery programs and 100,000 generated ones, with graph integrity checked after every operation.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Performance</h2>
  <BenchmarkSummary />
  <p>More: <a href={href("/benchmarks/")}>benchmarks and methodology</a>, <a href={href("/alien-signals-comparison/")}>alien-signals vs Reflex Quant</a>, <a href={href("/vue-reactivity-comparison/")}>Vue reactivity vs signals</a>.</p>

  <h2>Read next</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href={href("/javascript-signals-runtime/")}>JavaScript signals: what a reactive runtime actually needs</a></li>
    <li><a href={href("/reactive-runtime-anatomy/")}>Anatomy of a reactive engine</a>: how Quant is built, with diagrams</li>
    <li><a href={`${BLOB}/CONTRACT.md`}>The contract</a> (invariants and structural bounds)</li>
    <li><a href={`${BLOB}/research/LOG.md`}>Design log</a>: one counterexample per mechanism</li>
  </ul>
</Base>
